{
  "scope": "Literature prior parameters for the missing-extinction discrimination probes (plan Step 4). Every numeric value carries a pinned source (sha256 + printed location); rows marked unavailable carry null values and search notes, never defaults from memory. The probe consumes only AOD550, the broken-power-law Angstrom exponents (0.35-0.5 um mapped to the model profile's blue branch below the 500 nm break, 0.5-0.8 um to the visible branch above it) and the layer geometry (bottom/top/scale height); single scattering albedo and composition mixtures are tabulated by the source but are not consumable by the transmission-only probe and are therefore not transcribed. Fixed prior, never fitted, never a default: probe values enter counterfactual validation-side profiles only.",
  "source_convention": "Source file opac1998.pdf, sha256 68a20ba9488db1e0f65a9976d4ee46e6c879f89054fcff583ba67bb5d68d425c (121916 bytes, identical to the round2-pinned value; re-download verified 2026-11-11). Table 3 (printed page 840) tabulates tau(0.55 um) of the ten boundary-layer aerosol types at 80% relative humidity and of the background layers; its header note states the boundary-layer optical depths INCLUDE the free troposphere and the stratosphere, so the boundary-layer-only AOD550 is derived by subtracting the free-troposphere part (scaled to the type's boundary-layer thickness per Table 5) and the stratospheric 0.005. Angstrom exponents are tabulated for the 0.35-0.5 um and 0.5-0.8 um pairs (Eq. 4k, printed page 838). Table 5 (printed page 843) gives layer thickness H and scale height Z per type; the stratosphere is a 12-35 km layer with Z=99 km, the OPAC representation of a homogeneous layer (Sec. 5b, printed page 843).",
  "ft_part_scaling_formula": "ft_part(H_bl_km) = 0.013 * (exp(-H_bl/8) - exp(-12/8)) / (exp(-2/8) - exp(-12/8)); the free-troposphere composition (hence its 550 nm extinction per particle) is fixed for all types (Sec. 5b p. 842: 'The composition of the background aerosol types cannot be changed by the user'), only the layer boundaries vary per Table 5; the formula normalizes the tabulated 2-12 km optical depth 0.013 to a boundary-layer top at H_bl km.",
